# ============================================================================
# TWO-STEP PROBE SCENARIO (plans/cycle-1-craft-the-scenario/01-author.md)
# ROUND 4, CYCLE 2 on coolify - the 20th authored question on this repo counting
# every round. A RE-QUESTION routed back from measured runs.
#
# WHAT THIS DRAFT IS, IN ONE LINE
#   A deliberate RESTORATION of the question and the gold of scenario version
#   sha256:55ee5f19fe618a13 - the ONLY configuration in nineteen attempts that has landed
#   inside the window - with the two edits the last cycle made to it undone, each with the
#   number that killed it. The anchor is unchanged (ITERATE ON THE QUESTION, KEEP THE ANCHOR).
#
# SEAM PROFILE (quoted, `python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE Server --propose`)
#     app caller files : 45   (total incl test/non-app: 53)
#     SCATTER (dirs)   : 23
#     GREP-NOISE       : 'Server' in 295 app files   PRECISION=0.15  (lower=grep-hostile)
#     VERDICT          : SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
#   Two other candidates were profiled this session, same command:
#     Application       35 caller files, SCATTER 18, 'Application' in 142 files  PRECISION=0.25
#     StandaloneDocker   5 caller files, SCATTER  5, 'StandaloneDocker' in 26    PRECISION=0.19
#   StandaloneDocker cannot yield twelve dependent files from five callers; Application
#   profiles worse than Server on precision and scatter. Server keeps the anchor on the
#   ranking. Calibration on the record: 0.078 preceded a +1.00 cell, 0.78 was a deliberate
#   control tie, ~0.94 handed a baseline 17 of 18 rows in one grep. PRECISION RANKS, IT NEVER
#   KILLS - the two-arm run in the next phase decides.
#
#   Retention ring: `ring_sweep.py $CLONE --top 12` prints all twelve anchors at
#   rows=0 files=0 vias=0, "0 anchor(s) above the ranking threshold". So "what HOLDS X" is
#   off the menu on this repo and this is kind one, an occurrence-shaped ask. Said plainly
#   in the verdict notes rather than dressed up.
#
# THE AXIS SENTENCE
#   One identifying piece is lifted off a machine and kept somewhere the machine itself
#   cannot be carried to - a number stringified into a screen's own state and re-sent by the
#   browser, a bare int that is a helper's whole contract with its callers, a handle baked
#   into a cross-process queue lock key, a foreign provider's id interpolated into another
#   company's API, a handle copied into an outbound webhook body - and from that line on the
#   code trusts the piece to still resolve to the same machine when it comes back to it.
#
# WHAT THE ANSWER IS, THAT IS NOT A LIST OF THE PLACES A NAME APPEARS
#   A per-member RULING over a set that was established once and cannot grow: for each
#   member of the population fixed in step 1, either it keeps a piece - and then how far
#   that piece outlives the machine (one request, one stored row, or outside this product)
#   and what specifically starts being wrong the first time it stops resolving - or it is
#   the ground and gets one cheap line. Neither the lifetime nor the failure is carried by
#   an occurrence; both require the line to be read in its routine, and a member passed over
#   in silence is scored as a wrong answer rather than an omission. The SET underneath is
#   still occurrence-shaped, and that is stated in the notes.
#
# ============================================================================
# TRAJECTORY - every previous cycle on this repo, and which side of the window each fell on
#   The window: plain arm at or below 0.50 of `dependents` AND ours at least +0.50 above.
#   Numbers quoted from each run's own `scored.json` (`gold_recall.groups.dependents`),
#   swept this session over results/claude-opus-5/minibench/*/, cross-checked against
#   results/dryrun/coolify/cycles.{1,2,3}.jsonl and results/loop/coolify/cycles.jsonl.
#   Format: base -> sense.
#
#   ROUND 1 (cycles.1.jsonl)
#     1  "find EVERY place ... as a single subject"        0.9444 -> 1.0     CEILING (run-1 void)
#     2  anchor `instant_remote_process`, one hop back     0.8462 -> 0.8462  CEILING, delta 0
#     3  "every place that keeps a SCRAP of identity"      0.3571 -> 0.5714  FLOOR, delta short
#     4  "assume the whole of it changes ... ONE set"      0.9286 -> 0.5714  CEILING, losing
#     5  "the map is not the answer"                       0.0    -> 0.3333  FLOOR, our arm dead
#     6  "two passes, ground then kept"                    0.75   -> 0.9167  CEILING
#   ROUND 2 (cycles.2.jsonl)
#     1  "KEPT something after the machine was gone"       0.0    -> 0.75    FLOOR, delta short
#     2  same, without the written-down ground             0.0    -> 0.25    FLOOR, our arm dead
#     3  "at the far side of nearly every place"           0.9167 -> 0.8333  CEILING, losing
#     4  "something decided WHICH machine it would be"     0.8    -> 0.9333  CEILING
#     5  "never given a machine, never holds a stand-in"   0.9286 -> 1.0     CEILING
#     6  "what breaks - not the working ground"            0.7857 -> 0.7857  CEILING, delta 0
#   ROUND 3 (cycles.3.jsonl)
#     1  "what breaks" without the set framing             (void) -> 0.0     our arm dead
#     2  "a pass down the set, not a second search"        0.3333 -> 0.2     FLOOR, losing
#     3  same, tightened                                   0.2    -> 0.4667  FLOOR, delta short
#     4  "the set is fixed and has an edge"                0.3077 -> 1.0     INSIDE THE WINDOW
#     5  seven-step expansion, sense-only validation       (n/a)  -> 0.8462  SCENARIO
#     6  seven-step paid-gate validation                   0.5385 -> 0.6154  CEILING, DO-NOT-PAY
#   ROUND 4 (results/loop/coolify/cycles.jsonl)
#     1  step 1 cut to the contract, gold re-cut           0.5714 -> 0.4286  CEILING, losing
#
#   Nineteen cycles, one inside the window: ROUND 3 CYCLE 4, sha256:55ee5f19fe618a13,
#   quoted from its own scored.json this session -
#     baseline/run-1  4 of 13 = 0.3077     sense/run-1  13 of 13 = 1.0     delta +0.6923
#   and from results/dryrun/coolify/minibench.18.md, which read it: "Neither arm was cut off
#   ... The baseline's 0.308 is therefore its own result and not a censored one",
#   "cites_grounded 180 / cites_total 180 for sense, 120 / 120 for baseline".
#
# WHICH SIDE I AM CORRECTING FROM, AND HOW FAR I INTEND TO MOVE
#   From the CEILING side, and the whole distance back to a configuration that has already
#   been measured on the floor side - not one step past it. Round 4 cycle 1 made two edits to
#   the in-window scenario, both aimed at the SEVEN-STEP session's failure (validate.md), and
#   both are undone here because each one is measured to have cost the two-step probe:
#
#   (a) STEP 1'S POPULATION SWEEP IS RESTORED. Cycle 1 replaced "establish that whole
#       affected population ONCE, in a single sweep of the entire product and to its full
#       depth" with "this is the contract and not its reach: do not go looking for the places
#       that use it". Measured cost, quoted from results/loop/coolify/minibench.md: "The
#       three MCP calls, read from `sense-io.jsonl`, are `sense_search` ... `sense_status`,
#       and `sense_search` ... No `sense_blast`, no `sense_graph`", and
#       `transcript_miss.py`: "ADOPTION GAP - 1/1 runs made NO blast/graph call". The sense
#       arm fell 1.0 -> 0.4286. Step 1 asking for the population as ONE SET is what puts the
#       structural call in the session at all, and it is also the cost the baseline pays
#       before step 2 starts. It is the mechanism, not decoration.
#
#   (b) THE GOLD IS RESTORED, ALL THIRTEEN ROWS. Cycle 1 deleted every row the baseline had
#       cited and added nine rows whose credit line is a `server_id` column write. Measured
#       cost, quoted from results/loop/coolify/minibench.md: "Not one search that returned
#       the set - 51 greps of 65 calls", "`d:cleanup-execution-owner`,
#       `d:additional-network-pivot`, `d:clone-service-server-id`,
#       `d:new-destination-state-id` and `d:new-resource-state-id` all fall out of the
#       `server_id` sweep at call 10/60". The baseline rose 0.3077 -> 0.5714, across the
#       admission bar. The replacement rows were freer than the rows they replaced.
#
#   NOTHING ELSE MOVES, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. The four rows the baseline took at 0.3077
#   (`d:cloud-provider-external-id`, `d:cloud-status-lock-key`, `d:sentinel-event-uuid`,
#   `d:tunnel-event-id`) are NOT cut, though the last two cycles' reads both name them. Cut
#   them and the gold set has been re-cut twice off two runs per arm, which is exactly the
#   move that produced (b): the law says do not re-gold below ~5 runs per arm, "at n=2 one
#   row looked like a perfect discriminator (baseline 0/2) and was 4/5 by n=5". This cycle's
#   run makes the second independent observation on this gold. It is the observation that
#   licenses a re-gold; it is not the place to spend one.
#
#   The seven-step session's own failure is NOT addressed here and must not be: this phase
#   writes the two-step probe. What the expand phase needs is written down for it -
#   validate.md's mechanism read is that the seven-step's step 3 asked for the affected
#   population's "areas ... each named once with a single example located at file:line",
#   which is step 1 of THIS probe done a second time, so the baseline arrived at step 4
#   having already been paid for the sweep. The expansion has to spend that sweep once.
#
# MEMORIZATION (`memorization_probe.py $CLONE Server --json /tmp/memo-server.json`)
#   "closed-book recall 0.551", samples [0.0, 0.57, 0.551], truth_n 107, said_n 100 - the
#   model can recite much of the contract type's own member list. That is a fact about the
#   ANCHOR, which is a non-scoring group here, and MEMORIZATION IS A GOLD-TIME CONSTRAINT,
#   NEVER AN ADMISSION VERDICT. The clone is a single squashed commit, so churn dating is
#   uninformative on this repo and every scored row rests on the other leg of the law: each
#   is a line-level structural fact in a peripheral file - a stringify at :47, a pivot attach
#   at :130, a lock key at :30 - not a recitable API name. None of the thirteen scored
#   credits appears in the probe's recited member list.
#
# EVERY `dependents` ROW APPEARS IN THE SHOWN BLAST SET
#   `mcp_probe.py $CLONE '[{"name":"sense_blast","arguments":{"symbol":"Server"}}]'` at MCP
#   defaults, no min_confidence: 46 direct_callers + 89 affected_via_composition over 101
#   distinct files, `production_affected 169`. All thirteen `dependents` files and all four
#   `contract` files were intersected against that file list path by path this session and
#   every one is present.
#
# EVERY CREDIT WAS RE-READ FROM THE OPEN FILE THIS SESSION
#   All seventeen gold rows, and every secondary line each relation names, were printed out
#   of $CLONE at the stated line number and matched. Two wordings were corrected against
#   what the file actually says: `app/Models/Server.php:222` is `static::flushIdentityMap();`
#   inside the `static::updated(...)` closure opened at :221, and
#   `app/Models/ServerSetting.php:249` sits inside `public function server()` defined at
#   :247. No credit below is taken from a script tally.
#
#   Recorded, never a gate (NO GREP SCREEN IS A GATE, IN ANY FORM): a covering grep for the
#   identity tokens exists on this repo and the baseline has run it in every cycle. That
#   fact is written down here and decides nothing.
# ============================================================================
name: Coolify work session - audit the server contract before a rework
repo: coolify
contract_symbol: Server
contract_file: app/Models/Server.php

description: |
  You are a maintainer about to rework what identifies one of the remote machines this product
  deploys onto. Today a machine is a row with a number, a short handle, a display name and a
  network address, and code all over the product treats any one of those as a durable way of
  getting back to it later. The rework breaks that: the record is replaced wholesale, so a
  machine can be renamed, re-addressed, retired and stood in for by a different one, and a
  piece of identity taken today is no longer guaranteed to mean the same machine when it is
  read back tomorrow.
  Before you touch it you have to know two things: how a machine's identity is established,
  changed and torn down today, and every place that kept one of those pieces past the moment
  it had the machine itself - the places that will silently point at the wrong machine, or at
  no machine, without a single line of them changing.
  Throughout, an answer that points at a place must give an exact file:line a teammate could
  jump straight to, plus one phrase on why it matters. A bare filename does not count, a set
  of names offered together with one shared reason and no location on any of them does not
  count, and a missed dependent is a regression shipped.

steps:
  - name: Map the server contract
    prompt: >
      Task: orient, and be exact - the rest of the ticket is built on this and nothing later
      will send you back for it.
      Start by fixing the subject. Exactly one type in this product stands for one of these
      machines, and the whole ticket is about that one type and nothing else. Name it and give
      the file:line where it is defined, before anything else, and treat it from here on as a
      single subject rather than as a word to look for.
      Then two things about it, and the first is the larger.
      First, the identity itself, traced end to end. What identifies one of these machines;
      where the identifying fields are set as the record is written; where a lookup by identity
      is served out of a per-request cache rather than the store, what empties that cache and
      when; where a freshly created machine causes further records to be seeded from it,
      including any that take a COPY of an identifying field rather than a link back to it; and
      what the removal path does, in order, and what it does not clean up behind it. Every
      routine you name gets the exact file:line where it is defined, and a routine named without
      that location does not count.
      Second, and briefly: what a change to that one type would land on, as a shape and not as
      a roll. Assume the type itself changes under the product. I am not asking what it uses -
      the store it is read from, the connection helper it drives, the settings beside it - none
      of that is affected by its own change and none of it is what I want. I am asking the
      other direction: everything in this product that would have to be re-examined BECAUSE
      that type changed under it. That population has a near edge and a far side, and the far
      side is the part of it I will be paying for later. The near edge is the code that writes
      that type's name and is handed one of these machines; it is real, and it is the smaller
      and easier part. Behind it, and reached only through it, is code that was handed
      something a near-edge place took off a machine and passed on - that code never writes the
      type's name anywhere, it is affected all the same, and it is further out than one step
      from the type. An answer that stops at the near edge is not this answer, and stopping
      there is the specific way this part gets failed.
      Establish that whole affected population ONCE, in a single sweep of the entire product
      and to its full depth, not assembled a search at a time. Then report only its shape: the
      areas it lands in, each named once with a single example located at file:line, and how
      far out from the type it reaches. Do not enumerate the places themselves: I am not asking
      for that list here and it earns nothing. What I do want is the population established as
      one set, in a form you can work straight down afterwards, because the second part of this
      ticket is a pass over it and you will not be given the budget to go looking twice.
      Then say, in a few sentences, what a rework of identity would have to preserve for the
      first half of this answer to keep meaning what it means today.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "10"
        required: true
        description: The identity lifecycle traced through the routines that establish, cache, seed and tear it down, each located at file:line, plus the shape of the population a change to that type would land on - the areas it reaches, near edge and far side, named once each with one located example
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_graph
        required: false
        layer: adoption

  - name: Audit every dependent of the server contract
    prompt: >
      Task: this is the core of the ticket, and you are working it with the budget the first
      part already cost you. You already established the reach of that one type as a set, in one
      go, and that set is a fixed thing with an edge to it - it is not going to grow now. This
      part is a pass straight down it, in the order you established it, with a judgement at each
      line inside it. It is not a second search, and starting again with fresh words is how this
      gets half done. Take it as settled that the whole record is being replaced, and answer the
      only question that matters after that: what breaks.
      Not the code that is handed one of these machines and works with it while it has it in
      front of it. Whatever share of the product that runs, it will be handed the new record and
      it will be fine, and it is not what I am asking for. What breaks is every place that took
      one identifying piece off a machine, kept that piece after the machine itself was gone
      from its hands, and later trusts it to still mean the same machine when it comes back to
      it - a request later, a restart later, or in another system entirely. That trust is
      exactly what this rework withdraws, and not one of those places changes by a single line
      when it does.
      Test each candidate at the line itself, and only there: is a piece being lifted off a
      machine here and put somewhere the machine itself cannot be carried to, or read back out
      of somewhere after the machine was gone? If instead a machine came in and the code acted
      on it while it had it, that is the ground, and the ground is out.
      One thing about how these places hide, because it decides how you work: almost none of
      them writes the name of that type anywhere near the line that matters. The piece was
      taken off a machine by something else, handed on, and by the time it lands here it is a
      plain value sitting in the middle of a job about something else entirely - so the name
      you fixed in the first part will not lead you to them, and looking for it harder will not
      either. They are inside the reach you already established; they are the part of it where
      the machine itself did not travel.
      How this part is judged, because it is not by how good the places you hand me are. It is
      judged on whether the pass reached the end of the set. Every member of the set you
      established gets a ruling out of you, and there are exactly two rulings it can get. Either
      a piece is being kept there, and it gets the three parts below in full; or it is the
      ground, and it gets its location and the few words that say why it is the ground, and
      nothing more - that side is meant to be cheap, so spend almost nothing on it. A member you
      simply passed over in silence is not an omission, it is a wrong answer, because from here
      it cannot be told apart from one you never reached at all. And do not filter by how sure
      you are: a member you are uncertain about is ruled with the doubt written beside it, never
      dropped. Handing me a short list of the ones you were confident about, with the rest of
      the set unaccounted for, is the specific way this part gets failed - as is closing while a
      member of it is still unruled and you still have working time in front of you.
      For each kept place the answer has three parts and only three: the exact file:line where
      the piece is taken, kept or read back, and the routine that line sits in; how long that
      piece outlives the machine it came from - inside one request, inside one stored record, or
      outside this product entirely; and one phrase on what this place starts getting wrong the
      first time it no longer resolves to the machine it came from. Keep it short: I want the
      coverage, the lifetime and the failure, not the prose.
      Present the kept places organised by area, one section per area, and treat an area as
      finished only when every place inside it is listed separately with its own location; then
      close with the ruled-out members in set order, one line each. If you genuinely run out of
      time, say where in the set you stopped and what was still below that point, rather than
      closing on what you have. A file:line with no lifetime and no failure is half an answer,
      and a group summarised instead of listed reads as a group with nothing in it.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "6"
        required: true
        description: A ruling on every member of the population established in the first part - each identity-keeping place located at file:line with the routine it sits in, the lifetime of the piece it keeps and what that place gets wrong once the piece no longer resolves, and each ruled-out member accounted for in one line rather than passed over
      - type: no_grep
        value: no_grep
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption

scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.7
    efficiency: 0.3


gold:
  # ---- ANCHORS: the contract centre, its identity map, its obvious write path and its removal
  # ---- path. Step 1's answer. Both arms reach these and they do NOT score. Every line below
  # ---- was opened and read this session. None of these four files appears in `dependents`.
  - id: contract-model
    group: contract
    match: [Models/Server.php]
    relation: "app/Models/Server.php:230 `public static function findCached($id): ?static` - identity resolved out of a per-request identity map keyed on the machine's number, flushed by :222 `static::flushIdentityMap();` inside the `static::updated(...)` closure opened at :221, with the flush itself defined at :251 `public static function flushIdentityMap(): void`; the identity fields themselves are what every scored row keeps a piece of"
  - id: c:server-setting
    group: contract
    match: [Models/ServerSetting.php]
    relation: "app/Models/ServerSetting.php:59 `'server_id',` in the fillable list and :249 `return $this->belongsTo(Server::class);` inside `public function server()` defined at :247, - the companion record seeded from a freshly created machine, holding the machine's number rather than the machine; the obvious first hop off the contract and reached by both arms in every cycle"
  - id: c:server-delete
    group: contract
    match: [Server/Delete.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Server/Delete.php:34 `public function delete($password, $selectedActions = [])` ending at :60 `$this->server->delete();` - the removal path, the moment after which every piece kept elsewhere resolves to nothing"
  - id: c:validate-server
    group: contract
    match: [Server/ValidateServer.php]
    relation: "app/Actions/Server/ValidateServer.php:26 `public function handle(Server $server)` - the machine handed in and worked on with it in hand, the canonical shape of the ground that step 2 excludes"

  # ---- DEPENDENTS: the scored group. THIRTEEN FILES, TWELVE AREAS, one row per file, and
  # ---- the set is restored unchanged from sha256:55ee5f19fe618a13, the one configuration
  # ---- measured inside the window (baseline 4 of 13 = 0.3077, sense 13 of 13 = 1.0). No row
  # ---- is added, cut or reworded on a citation rate this cycle: the last re-gold was made
  # ---- at two runs per arm and it moved the baseline from 0.3077 to 0.5714, so this run is
  # ---- the second independent observation and not the place to spend one.
  # ---- Every one of the thirteen appears in the shown `sense_blast` payload for the
  # ---- contract, intersected path by path this session against its 101 distinct files.
  # ---- None of the thirteen appears in the `contract` group. At every scored line there is
  # ---- no machine in hand: only a piece taken off one earlier and kept somewhere the
  # ---- machine itself cannot be carried to. Five of the thirteen do sit under a
  # ---- Server-named directory (Sentinel, CaCertificate, CloudProviderToken, Proxy, the
  # ---- cloud-provider job) and they stay: TOKEN-DARKNESS IS NOT THE MECHANISM, and a
  # ---- banked +0.72 cell had 16 of 16 gold files carrying the anchor token.

  # -- area: outbound notification payloads
  - id: d:container-webhook-handle
    group: dependents
    match: [Container/ContainerStopped.php]
    relation: "app/Notifications/Container/ContainerStopped.php:114 `'server_uuid' => $this->server->uuid,` inside `toWebhook()` at :106 - the machine's handle copied into the body of an outbound webhook that leaves this product entirely, so the piece lives in someone else's system and can never be corrected from here"

  # -- area: global search component state
  - id: d:global-search-state-id
    group: dependents
    match: [Livewire/GlobalSearch.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/GlobalSearch.php:1208 `$this->selectedServerId = $serverId;` inside `selectServer()` at :1206 - a picked machine reduced to a number kept in the component's state, re-sent by the browser on every later request and resolved again at :1218 `Server::ownedByCurrentTeam()->find($this->selectedServerId)` inside `loadDestinations()` at :1215"

  # -- area: destination creation wizard
  - id: d:new-destination-state-id
    group: dependents
    match: [Destination/New/Docker.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Destination/New/Docker.php:47 `$this->serverId = (string) $this->selectedServer->id;` inside `mount()` at :35 - the number stringified into component state and resolved back on a later request, so the machine is gone from its hands between the two halves of the form"

  # -- area: resource creation wizard
  - id: d:new-resource-state-id
    group: dependents
    match: [Project/New/Select.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Project/New/Select.php:409 `$this->server_id = $server->id;` inside `setServer(Server $server)` at :407 - the number kept in wizard state across every subsequent request of the flow and written into the created records later, while the machine object itself is dropped at the end of the request"

  # -- area: shared project screens
  - id: d:additional-network-pivot
    group: dependents
    match: [Project/Shared/Destination.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Project/Shared/Destination.php:130 `$this->resource->additional_networks()->attach($mainDestination->id, ['server_id' => $mainDestination->server->id]);` inside `promote()` at :115 - the number written into a pivot row as a column of the join itself, read back later to decide which machines a resource additionally runs on"
  - id: d:logs-ownership-check
    group: dependents
    match: [Project/Shared/GetLogs.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Project/Shared/GetLogs.php:127 `if (! Server::ownedByCurrentTeam()->where('id', $this->server->id)->exists()) {` inside `getLogs()` at :125 - a component that already holds a machine throws the machine away and re-checks by its number on every poll, so the authorisation decision rides on the number rather than on the object it came from"

  # -- area: broadcast event listeners
  - id: d:tunnel-event-id
    group: dependents
    match: [CloudflareTunnelChangedNotification.php]
    relation: "app/Listeners/CloudflareTunnelChangedNotification.php:18 `$server_id = data_get($event, 'data.server_id');` inside `handle()` at :16 - a bare number arriving on a broadcast event raised by another process, resolved at :21 `Server::where('id', $server_id)->firstOrFail()`, so a number that no longer means the same machine takes the listener down rather than skipping"

  # -- area: global helper functions
  - id: d:compose-validate-id
    group: dependents
    match: [helpers/docker.php]
    relation: "bootstrap/helpers/docker.php:1252 `function validateComposeFile(string $compose, int $server_id): string|Throwable` resolving at :1255 `$server = Server::ownedByCurrentTeam()->find($server_id);` - the helper's whole contract with its callers is a bare int, so the machine is gone from every call site's hands before this function starts and `find()` yields null rather than an error when the number stops resolving"

  # -- area: proxy configuration screens
  - id: d:proxy-config-state-id
    group: dependents
    match: [Proxy/NewDynamicConfiguration.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Server/Proxy/NewDynamicConfiguration.php:30 `$this->server = Server::ownedByCurrentTeam()->whereId($this->server_id)->first();` inside `mount()` at :28, resolving the public property declared at :24 `public $server_id;` - the number arrives as component state set by the parent and re-sent by the browser, so every later request of this component starts from the number and not from a machine, and `first()` returns null rather than failing when it stops resolving"

  # -- area: certificate management
  - id: d:ca-cert-job-id
    group: dependents
    match: [CaCertificate/Show.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Server/CaCertificate/Show.php:82 `server_id: $this->server->id,` inside `dispatch(new RegenerateSslCertJob(...))` at :81 - the number is peeled off the machine and handed to a QUEUED job by name, so the piece outlives the request that dispatched it by however far the queue is behind, and the job regenerates a certificate against whatever machine that number resolves to when it finally runs"

  # -- area: cloud provider integration
  - id: d:cloud-provider-external-id
    group: dependents
    match: [CloudProviderToken/Show.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Server/CloudProviderToken/Show.php:117 `])->timeout(10)->get(\"https://api.hetzner.cloud/v1/servers/{$this->server->hetzner_server_id}\");` guarded at :114 `if ($this->server->hetzner_server_id) {` - a FOREIGN provider's id, stored on the machine's own row, interpolated into a call to another company's API; the piece has already left this product, nothing here can repoint it, and a replaced machine validates its token against a stranger's instance"

  # -- area: background jobs
  - id: d:cloud-status-lock-key
    group: dependents
    match: [ServerCloudProviderStatusCheckJob.php]
    relation: "app/Jobs/ServerCloudProviderStatusCheckJob.php:30 `return [(new WithoutOverlapping('server-cloud-provider-status-'.$this->server->uuid))->expireAfter(130)->dontRelease()];` inside `middleware()` at :28 - the handle baked into a queue lock key that lives in the cache across processes and restarts, so once the handle stops meaning the same machine the overlap guard silently stops guarding and two status checks run against one machine at once"

  # -- area: sentinel screens
